{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1523667-bloodlines-questions","id":1523667,"title":"Bloodlines questions.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-10-05T15:03:31-07:00","id":1523667,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Bloodlines questions.","content":"When it comes to bloodlines, can you only start with one if your sire has it?<br /> <br /> Otherwise, what is the process of getting one? I've read the VtR:2e rules and they are unclear to me...<br /> <br /> Thanks","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3379-space-chthuloid-1969","name":"Space Chthuloid 1969","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3379_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-05T17:06:17-07:00","id":1523670,"title":"","content":"In general you are embraced into your clan by your sire. You need someone to bring you into your bloodline, this requires you meet blood potency thresholds. Your sire can do it at Blood Potency 1, so at creation your sire can bring you in easy enough. <br /> <br /> If your sire won't, or isn't a member of the bloodline another vampire you are related to can do it at Blood Potency 2. Think your sire's sire, or another vampire your sire embraced and you're on the right track. It is possible to start with blood potency 2 by giving up 5 merit dots at character creation. <br /> <br /> At Blood Potency 4 any vampire of your clan can bring you into the bloodline. This blood potency is not typically available at character creation. <br /> <br /> And for completeness, at blood potency 6 you can make your own bloodline. <br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-06T01:41:34-07:00","id":1523686,"title":"","content":"Bloodlines suffer heavily in the Second Edition core by the crunch for space. They get a lot more explanation in the First Edition book.<br /> <br /> First, the bit of flavor that the book didn't have room for: every bloodline is not narratively equal. Some of them are like dynastic lineages that have become so distinct at a particular practice that the blood has adapted to it. Some of them are like secret societies and guarded secrets, primarily initiated by an Avus only for those deemed worthy. Some are explicitly <i>curses,</i> undesired by those who suffer them but naturally suffered and unable to be shed, though even these have their compensatory gifts. Some bloodlines you get, some bloodlines you seek, some bloodlines <i>nobody</i> wants. All of them share the core mechanics, but these manifest in different ways. The Morbus <i>can</i> act as Avus to induct a seeker into their bloodline, but no sane vampire would want to seek that out.<br /> <br /> The two standard ways to join a bloodline are through lineage or Avus. Lineage is being sired by a member of the bloodline. <u>You can join the bloodline for free, from chargen or any point thereafter.</u> While this is the player's decision, it isn't always the <i>character's</i> decision. Sometimes it takes practice to manifest the nature of the bloodline, but sometimes it's just how you are after the Embrace. Sometimes the curse might even manifest in <i>later</i> nights, perhaps roused by some kind of sympathetic stimulus. No mechanics for this; you just decide.<br /> <br /> If your sire isn't a member of the bloodline, you need an Avus, a member of the bloodline who is willing to induct you. (Most bloodlines are also specific to a particular clan and require you to be a member of the same clan.) At Blood Potency 2, you can receive an Avus who shares a blood tie up to three removes. So if any of your childer and grandchilder, your grandsire, or the other childer or grandchilder of your sire or your grandsire is a member of the bloodline, even if your actual sire isn't, they can Avus for you. <u>If you buy Blood Potency 2 with five of your starting Merit dots, then this can be part of your background in chargen.</u> At Blood Potency 4, anyone you can convince to Avus for you can do the job.<br /> <br /> A vampire without a bloodline at Blood Potency 6 can create a new bloodline through an act of will to reshape their own blood.<br /> <br /> The Blood Potency 4 and 6 options are, of course, <u>impossible to meet by a neonate fresh out of chargen</u> who hasn't been given bonus experiences by the Storyteller to buy those dots with...<br /> <br /> ...assuming that you are a neonate. With your Storyteller's permission, you might use the chargen rules to represent not a fresh neonate, but an older vampire who has been withered and lessened by a long period of torpor. This might result in a character who does not have Blood Potency 4 or 6, but who did in the past, and therefore could have acquired a bloodline through one of those means. A bloodline once manifested cannot be shed, leaving the change in the blood in place even after the vampire awakes from a long and turbulent torpor weak, out of practice, without old allies or resources, and with Blood Potency 1.<br /> <br /> Aside from that, the Chronicles of Darkness are filled with anomalous events not represented by the standard rules. As mentioned above, some bloodlines are <i>literal</i> curses that originate from a vampire <i>falling victim to a curse.</i> No mechanics represent this, but if you think you have an appropriate idea for something bizarre and supernatural your character might have lived (or unlived) through that might spontaneously manifest as a bloodline in a way not understood by vampires in the setting, talk to your Storyteller and see if they agree.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}